# Local HTTPS / HTTP-2 terminator in front of the dev API (apps/api on :3005).
#
# Why: every ElectricSQL shape long-poll hits the API origin. Over HTTP/1.1 the browser caps
# a single origin at 6 concurrent connections, so once a page holds 6+ live shapes (or shapes
# + HMR + asset loads) they starve each other and dev feels frozen. Terminating TLS here gives
# the browser HTTP/2 multiplexing to the API — effectively unlimited concurrent shape streams.
# Ref: https://electric.ax/docs/sync/guides/troubleshooting  (slow shapes / slow HMR / slow dev)
#
# Scope: only the API needs this — the frontend Vite dev servers keep their own origins on
# plain http://localhost:300x (their assets/HMR don't exhaust the budget the way live shapes do).
#
# Usage (run Caddy on the host — NOT in Docker; containers break HTTP/2):
#   caddy trust            # one-time: trust Caddy's local CA (needs admin / UAC)
#   caddy run              # auto-loads this Caddyfile from the repo root
# Then point the apps at the proxy instead of the raw API:
#   VITE_API_URL=https://localhost:3443     (apps/*/.env)
#
# We rewrite the upstream Host back to the API's own origin so Better Auth sees a request host
# that matches BETTER_AUTH_URL (http://localhost:3005) — keeps auth working with OR without the
# proxy, and leaves the cookie host-only on `localhost` (shared across every localhost port).

# Move our admin endpoint off the default :2019 so this instance can coexist with any other
# Caddy already running on the machine (e.g. another project's dev proxy). We only serve the
# single https://localhost:3443 site, so also skip the automatic HTTP->HTTPS redirect listener
# (it would try to bind :80, which is already taken on Windows by http.sys / the other Caddy).
{
	admin localhost:2020
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

https://localhost:3443 {
	reverse_proxy localhost:3005 {
		header_up Host localhost:3005
	}
}
